<urn:atm:triage:finding/ARCH-BINDADDR-001-AK>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "ARCH-BINDADDR-001-AK" ;
  triage:title "peer_https_interfaces persists a volatile local bind IP as durable identity instead of a stable host identity" ;
  triage:description "peer_https_interfaces.bind_addr (crates/atm-storage-rusqlite/src/shared_db.rs:96, `bind_addr TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY`) stores a literal SocketAddr as the row's durable primary key. atm-daemon binds this stored address verbatim at startup (crates/atm-daemon/src/https_transport.rs:341 and the duplicate composition path at crates/atm-runtime/src/composition.rs:135, both `TcpListener::bind(interface.bind_addr)`), and the storage contract encodes bind_addr as the interface's identity (crates/atm-storage/src/contract.rs:721 `remove_interface(&self, bind_addr: SocketAddr)`; write path in crates/atm-storage-rusqlite/src/peer_config_store.rs:61-67 upserts ON CONFLICT(bind_addr)). When the host's local address changes (DHCP renewal, VPN connect/disconnect, network switch), the persisted bind_addr becomes invalid and the daemon fails to bind at startup -- confirmed live: daemon startup blocked after the local IP changed away from a stored 10.10.100.64, logged repeatedly in ~/.atm/daemon/atm-daemon-1.3.1.stderr.log as 'daemon runtime assembly is unavailable; atm-daemon startup is blocked'. Root cause per user analysis: this conflates durable configuration (operator intent: which interfaces should be enabled) with runtime network state (the current resolved local address), by making the volatile address itself the durable key. ADR-040 (Peer Authority Resolution) already establishes the correct pattern for the symmetric peer-trust case: durable records store a stable hostname + pin and resolve to a current address only at connect time, never persisting a resolved IP alias. ADR-034 (Minimal Cross-Host HTTPS Transport) states 'Interface bindings ... are durable configuration behind the storage trait' for the local listener side without carrying that same resolve-at-startup principle over from ADR-040 -- the local bind interface never inherited the fix its peer-trust sibling already has. Correct remediation is architectural, not a one-line patch: persist a stable host identity (e.g. advertise_host, already present as a column) as the durable key/config, and have the daemon resolve/select its current local bind address dynamically at startup, analogous to ADR-040's bounded-resolution model. This requires an ADR-level decision (amend ADR-034 or add an ADR-040-style rule scoped to local bind identity) before a code fix is dispatched, since the current schema (bind_addr as PRIMARY KEY) and TcpListener::bind(interface.bind_addr) call sites are downstream of that design choice, not the root cause themselves." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-ak" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "architecture" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "repo_wide" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-08-05T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AK.Ops ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-08-05T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
